1: # P3F.31. The OS49 positive-diagonal filling and its actual page
2: 
3: **Proved.** This statement concerns the positive-diagonal filling
4: defined below. It leaves the negative-diagonal family of P3F.26 intact.
5: The two fans have the common torus family and are related by the
6: specified toric flop; they are not identified as the same family.
7: 
8: This positive-diagonal filling gives an actual sphere family over
9: \(\lambda=r^3\), with zero positive-loop return index. The actual
10: page, circle-line comparison, boundary support, and cap marking
11: are constructed below. Root and fourfold independently audited
12: these maps and the relative cohomology argument before this
13: statement was marked proved.
14: 
15: ## 1. The exact line subfan
16: 
17: Keep the periods \(h=(2,1)\), \(g=(-3,-1)\) of P3F.26 and use
18: the periodic triangulation whose two triangles in a unit square are
19: \[
20:  (0,0),(1,0),(1,1),\qquad (0,0),(0,1),(1,1).          \tag{P3F.31.1}
21: \]
22: All cones at height one are unimodular, and the full period lattice
23: has index one. The compact fundamental domains of P3F.26 prove
24: smoothness, compactness and properness of this actual new filling.
25: The finite cap and the type-II line-complement quotient are unchanged.
26: Its integral topology can be compared by actual morphisms. Put
27: \(u_j\) for the height-one rays over the four corners of a unit
28: square. The common refinement adds the ray
29: \[
30:  v=u_1+u_2=u_0+u_4=(1,1,2).
31: \]
32: It is the blowup of the diagonal \(\mathbb P^1\) in either
33: small resolution. The normal bundle of each centre is
34: \(\mathcal O(-1)^{\oplus2}\), and the exceptional divisor is
35: \(\mathbb P^1\times\mathbb P^1\), with the two morphisms
36: contracting its two rulings. The equivariant period identifications
37: give these global morphisms on the two compact fillings; there is
38: one orbit of diagonal curves.
39: 
40: For this blowup, integral cohomology is the direct sum of pullback
41: cohomology and the Gysin image of \(H^{q-2}(\mathbb P^1;\mathbb Z)\).
42: One can verify the splitting by the long exact sequence of the
43: complement of the centre and the Thom isomorphism; integration
44: on the exceptional ruling, whose exceptional-divisor degree is
45: \(-1\), proves that the added generator is primitive. Thus the
46: only additional groups are one free generator in degrees two and
47: four. Van Kampen preserves the fundamental group: both local
48: pieces and their sphere-bundle boundary are simply connected.
49: Apply these facts to the two morphisms from the same refinement.
50: Since the negative-diagonal filling of P3F.26 is a simply connected
51: integral homology sphere, so is this positive-diagonal filling.
52: It is therefore the standard smooth six-sphere. This comparison
53: does not identify the two families' return maps.
54: 
55: Here is the additional property needed for the page. On the infinite
56: Tate chain of the source \(I_2\), put
57: \[
58:  h_j=\lceil j/2\rceil,
59:  \qquad h_{j+2}=h_j+1.
60: \]
61: The fan of the actual line complement \(M^\times\) has rays
62: \((j,h_j,1)\) and cones on consecutive pairs of these rays.
63: Projection to \((j,1)\) is the source Tate-chain projection. On
64: each such cone the total space is \(\mathbb C^2\times\mathbb C^*\);
65: the last factor is the delta circle's complexification. The line
66: degree on component \(j\) is
67: \[
68:  -(h_{j-1}+h_{j+1}-2h_j).
69: \]
70: The minus sign follows directly from fibre-coordinate transitions:
71: if the two adjacent slopes are \(s_-,s_+\), the fibre coordinates
72: on the two component charts differ by \(Z^{s_--s_+}\), which is
73: the inverse of the line-frame transition. The degrees are therefore
74: \((-1,+1)\), exactly those of \(M=\mathcal O(G-O)\).
75: The edges are alternately \((1,1)\) and \((1,0)\), so this is a
76: literal subfan of (P3F.31.1). Its quotient by \(h\) is the actual
77: \(M^\times\) over the whole source \(I_2\) neighbourhood,
78: including both nodes. This identifies its map into the filling by
79: toric charts, not merely by a boundary lattice.
80: 
81: It also checks the direction of the lift. Under \(g\), the graph
82: height defect is
83: \[
84:  h_j-1-h_{j-3}=0\quad(j\text{ even}),\qquad
85:  h_j-1-h_{j-3}=1\quad(j\text{ odd}).
86: \]
87: Thus the lift is invertible over source \(C_0\) and acquires its
88: simple zero over source \(C_1\), which is sent to target \(C_0\).
89: This is precisely the target-point linearization
90: \(\phi:T^*M\to M\) of P3F.28. Holomorphic units are retained
91: in the line frames and in both periods; they do not change any of
92: these chart inclusions.
93: 
94: For comparison, the negative-diagonal fan of P3F.26 has horizontal,
95: vertical, and negative-diagonal edges, and does not contain this
96: positive-diagonal line subfan. Flipping the diagonal of each unit
97: square gives the actual toric birational comparison, through the
98: common refinement. No diffeomorphism or equality of return indices
99: is concluded from that comparison here.
100: 
101: ## 2. A page carrying the actual source line
102: 
103: Choose a Hermitian norm on the source line which, on the regular
104: annulus bounding the cusp neighbourhood, is unitary flat on every
105: elliptic fibre. Such fibrewise metrics vary smoothly on that annulus;
106: interpolate their logarithms to any smooth metric on a smaller
107: neighbourhood of the central fibre. No flat extension across the
108: nonzero component degrees is imposed. The lift covering
109: \(-Q\), before the zero is removed, is a global holomorphic line
110: map on the compact source. Its operator norm is bounded. After
111: choosing \(|\lambda|\) sufficiently small, it decreases every
112: nonzero norm on its domain by a factor bounded above by a constant
113: \(c<1\). On a source cusp neighbourhood choose three radii
114: \(R_1<R_2<R_3\) with \(cR_3<R_1\). The three norm shells
115: \(\|v\|=R_j\) map to the filling through the actual line subfan.
116: No nonzero iterate of the contracting lift identifies two points
117: of those shells: every forward iterate that stays in the line
118: complement has norm at most \(cR_3\), and the inverse case is
119: the same assertion reversed. A zero cannot leave this open subset
120: and re-enter it through a later toric translate. Indeed the exact
121: height defect of \(g^N\), for \(N>0\), is
122: \[
123:  h_j-N-h_{j-3N}=
124:  \begin{cases}\lfloor N/2\rfloor&j\text{ even},\\
125:  \lceil N/2\rceil&j\text{ odd}.
126:  \end{cases}
127: \]
128: It is positive after the first zero; the same cone calculation
129: at a source node uses both endpoint defects. Thus no later
130: positive translate is a point of the line-complement subfan.
131: The period \(h\) has already been divided out in the
132: source line bundle. The resulting maps are therefore injective
133: immersions. On a compact closed cusp neighbourhood the shells
134: are compact, so these maps are embeddings.
135: 
136: Their delta quotients are three embedded copies of the actual
137: source \(I_2\) neighbourhood. Each misses the fixed sphere,
138: because the delta action on \(M^\times\) is free and the toric
139: open subset just specified misses the vertical fixed edge. On
140: each copy the actual delta bundle is exactly the chosen unit
141: circle bundle of \(M\), by the displayed map. This gives the
142: line identification through both nodes and both components.
143: 
144: Away from the cusp use the marked regular-binding page of the
145: affine circle quotient. The order-three finite cap has gamma
146: displacement \(1/3\); the corresponding circle base is the
147: weighted Hopf three-sphere of weights \((1,3)\). Its regular
148: binding page projects with degree three to the elliptic base,
149: branched at the order-three value and at the regular binding.
150: The type-II neighbourhood uses the original line-complement
